Exercise 1: Extracting and Cropping Documents from a "Digital Archive"
Date: December 15, 2025
Background
Your goal is to write automated code that identifies the boundaries separating different documents within a scanned image and crops them into separate images.
Exercise Objectives

Implement edge detection algorithms.
Use the Hough Transform to detect straight lines, utilizing the code implemented in the class exercise. Do not use OpenCV.
Manage tabular data using the Pandas library.
Perform array manipulation (slicing) to crop images.

Input Data
You have access to files containing scans of documents (images attached to the exercise). These images feature textures of old paper and stains.
Execution Instructions (Step-by-Step)
You must write a program in Python that performs the following steps:

Loading and Preprocessing
Load the image.
Convert the image to grayscale.
Apply noise filtering if necessary.

Edge Detection
Use the Canny algorithm (or a similar one learned in class) to generate a Binary Edge Map.
Ensure the threshold is adjusted so that only strong contours (the boundaries between documents) are preserved.
You may need to apply morphological operations before or after, but use them minimally.

Line Detection Using Hough Transform
This is the core of the exercise. Use the HoughLines code from the class exercise to find straight lines in the image – specifically, the frames of the "documents" in each image.
Tune the parameters (rho, theta, threshold, minLineLength) so that the algorithm captures the lines that separate the documents.

Data Management with Pandas
Store the coordinates of the detected lines (x1, y1, x2, y2) in a Pandas DataFrame.
Display the DataFrame as intermediate output (for debugging and verification purposes).

Image Cropping
Based on the lines found in the DataFrame, calculate the cropping points.
Save the images with the detected lines drawn on them.


